\section{Discussion and Conclusion}
\label{sec:discussion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

\looseness=-1
\paragraph{Practitioner decision framework.}
Three zero-cost diagnostics predict self-verification failure: $K_\text{eff} \leq 1.3$, SC $< 40\%$ ($-2.83$\,pp), and $C_0 < 5\%$ (degenerates to majority voting). Only the intermediate regime (SC $\sim$50--60\%, $K_\text{eff} > 1.8$) offers marginal gain.

\looseness=-1
\paragraph{Design principles for escaping SCB.}
SCB extends beyond SICA to any method extracting structured representations---self-verification \citep{huang2024selfcorrect}, self-refinement \citep{madaan2023selfrefine}, chain-of-verification \citep{dhuliawala2023chainofverification} all face the same distributional prior. Three conditions appear necessary for escaping the symbolic mirage. (i)~\emph{Structural independence}: the verifier must not share architecture or training data with the generator (debate: $\kappa = 0.15$, $-18.6$\,pp). (ii)~\emph{Positive capability gap}: combo gain decreases monotonically with generator capability (Table~\ref{tab:cross_generator_nli}), significant up to SC $\approx 70\%$ but vanishing above $74\%$. (iii)~\emph{Task-verifier alignment}: all three NLI architectures produce gains at $w \geq 3$ (Appendices~\ref{app:capability-gap},~\ref{app:extended-discussion}).

\section{Cross-Architecture Proof-of-Concept}
\label{sec:solutions}
\label{sec:cross-arch}

\paragraph{Method.}
\looseness=-1 We test with DeBERTa-large \citep{he2021deberta} (406M, encoder-only), fine-tuned on MultiNLI \citep{williams2018mnli}, classifying (premise, conclusion) pairs as entailment, contradiction, or neutral. This proof-of-concept is limited to propositional-logic benchmarks whose premise-conclusion structure naturally suits NLI; generalization to non-logical domains remains untested. In combo mode, each NLI-supporting vote counts $w$ times alongside SC's $K$ votes. We evaluate on Mistral-7B FOLIO ($n = 204$) and ProofWriter depth-5 ($n = 600$).

\paragraph{Main result.}
\looseness=-1 DeBERTa NLI alone achieves 49.51\% on FOLIO (below SC's 55.88\%) and 55.00\% on ProofWriter (above SC's 39.33\%). On FOLIO ($n = 204$), combo $w{=}3$ yields $+2.94$\,pp (n.s.). On ProofWriter ($n{=}600$), combo $w{=}3$ achieves significant gains for Mistral-7B ($+6.67$\,pp, $p < 0.001$) and LLaMA-8B ($+4.17$\,pp, $p = 0.004$); stronger generators show no improvement---while same-model SICA \emph{degrades} ProofWriter ($-2.83$\,pp; Table~\ref{tab:main_results}), illustrating that corrective signal must originate outside the model's distribution (\S\ref{sec:mirage}). After BH correction ($m{=}12$), Mistral-7B ($w{=}3$, $w{=}5$) and LLaMA-8B ($w{=}1$, $w{=}3$) remain significant.

\paragraph{Why it works.}
\looseness=-1 Our results suggest three conditions jointly enable the escape---structural independence, positive capability gap, and task-verifier alignment---expanded in \S\ref{sec:discussion}. NLI classification operates on (premise, conclusion) pairs, a different task from constraint extraction; the success may partly reflect task-format alignment. All three NLI architectures produce gains at $w \geq 3$.

\paragraph{Capability gap hypothesis.}
\looseness=-1 Four generators spanning a 46-point SC range on ProofWriter ($n{=}600$), all paired with DeBERTa-large-mnli (55.00\% standalone; Table~\ref{tab:cross_generator_nli}): Mistral-7B (SC 39.33\%, below the verifier) gains $+6.67$\,pp ($p < 0.001$) and LLaMA-8B (SC 70.00\%, above the verifier) gains $+4.17$\,pp ($p = 0.004$); generators above SC $\approx 74\%$ show no improvement. Per-label analysis confirms complementary error patterns that diminish as the generator improves (Appendix~\ref{app:multi-nli}). The monotonic relationship holds across three NLI architectures and six verifier-generator pairs (Figure~\ref{fig:capability-gap}; Appendix~\ref{app:capability-gap}). A $2{\times}2$ generator${\times}$extractor matrix confirms BR $> 1$ in all eight cells ($1.94$--$2.70$; Appendix~\ref{app:2x2}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-generator NLI escape with DeBERTa-large-mnli verifier (55.00\% standalone, ProofWriter $n{=}600$). Combo gain decreases monotonically with generator capability; gains are significant for generators with SC $\leq 70\%$ ($w{=}3$).}
\label{tab:cross_generator_nli}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{\begin{tabular}{@{}llcccc@{}}
\toprule
Generator & Family & SC & Gap & Combo $\Delta$ ($w{=}3$) & $p$ \\
\midrule
Mistral-7B & Mistral & 39.33\% & $+$15.67 & $+$6.67\,pp & $<$0.001 \\
LLaMA-3.1-8B & LLaMA & 70.00\% & $-$15.00 & $+$4.17\,pp & 0.004 \\
Qwen2.5-14B & Qwen & 73.50\% & $-$18.50 & $+$0.83\,pp & 0.405 \\
Qwen3-14B & Qwen & 85.83\% & $-$30.83 & $+$0.50\,pp & 0.45 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Disagreement-Routed Cascade.}
A cost-aware variant invokes NLI only below consensus threshold $\tau$, recovering 90\% of full-combo gain at ${\sim}$35\% NLI cost ($\tau{=}0.7$; Appendix~\ref{app:drc}).

\section{Analysis}
\label{sec:analysis}

\looseness=-1 We test the three predictions of the symbolic mirage hypothesis (\S\ref{sec:mirage}).

\subsection{Condorcet Effective-K}
\label{sec:effective-k}

\looseness=-1 Table~\ref{tab:independence} (Appendix) reports Fleiss' $\kappa$ and Effective-K (Eq.~\ref{eq:effk}) across 15 conditions at $K = 12$. Among instruction-tuned models, $\kappa$ ranges from 0.21 to 0.82, yielding $K_{\text{eff}} = 1.20\text{--}3.62$ as an ordinal diagnostic: higher $K_{\text{eff}}$ indicates greater vote diversity, though it should not be interpreted as a literal effective sample size (\S\ref{sec:condorcet}). Across four Mistral-7B seeds, $\kappa \in [0.41, 0.51]$ yet $\Delta$ ranges from $+3.43$ to $-0.49$\,pp, indicating seed-level constraint quality matters beyond independence. Positive $\Delta$ requires both sufficient $K_{\text{eff}}$ ($\leq 1.30$ never improves) and SC accuracy ($K_{\text{eff}} = 3.62$ with SC $= 39\%$ degrades). All four model families produce $\kappa \geq 0.41$ (Table~\ref{tab:kappa_generalization}).


\subsection{Symbolic Confirmation Bias}
\label{sec:confirmation-bias}

\looseness=-1 Self-extracted constraints systematically align with the model's majority answer. On Mistral-7B FOLIO, when SICA errs, 83.3\% of constraints support the incorrect answer---bias ratio $4.97\times$ (Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-bias})---and 94\% of errors overlap with SC. A shuffle control reveals this bias is \emph{structural}: randomizing constraint--answer associations while preserving per-trace counts yields $\text{BR}_\text{null} \approx \text{BR}_\text{obs}$ ($p > 0.7$), indicating that BR reflects the trace-count distribution rather than extraction-specific error. Z3 rejects only 3.4\% of constraints, and MAX-SAT produces answers identical to count-only aggregation on 94.4\% of problems on Mistral-7B FOLIO (Table~\ref{tab:ablation}), confirming that the formal verification layer is vacuous (Eq.~\ref{eq:degenerate}): 99\% of constraints pass Z3 validation yet add no information beyond majority voting. This vacuity is $\alpha$-insensitive: agreement $\geq$99\% across $\alpha \in \{0.1, \ldots, 0.9\}$ for both high- and moderate-BR conditions (Table~\ref{tab:alpha-ablation}).

\looseness=-1 This pattern generalizes: $4.97\times$ (Mistral), $3.93\times$ (Qwen-14B), $2.88\times$ (LLaMA), all $> 1.0$ with 95\% bootstrap CIs (Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-bias}). Cross-model extraction confirms SCB universality across nine models from 7B to frontier (BR $1.66$--$4.97\times$; Table~\ref{tab:frontier-scb}). BR $> 1$ further holds across all eight cells of a $2{\times}2$ generator${\times}$extractor matrix on FOLIO and ProofWriter (range $1.94$--$2.70$; Appendix~\ref{app:2x2}). Constraints use trace-specific variables (56.4\% zero cross-answer overlap), collapsing aggregation to majority voting.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\caption{Symbolic confirmation bias across nine models on FOLIO ($n = 204$). BR: mean per-problem bias ratio. $n_\text{err}$: number of incorrect instances on which BR is computed. $\Delta$: SICA $-$ SC (pp). Ext.: $^a$self-extraction; $^b$cross-model (Mistral-7B extracts FOL from target traces). $^*$Interim ($n = 95/204$). All models exhibit $\text{BR} > 1$; BR estimates for high-accuracy models ($n_\text{err} < 40$) have wider confidence intervals; Gemini ($n_\text{err} \approx 12$) should be interpreted with particular caution. Process-level similarity ($\kappa_{\text{proc}}$) ranges from $0.25$ (R1-Distill) to $0.79$ (gpt-4o/4.1/Gemini); see Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-process}.}
\label{tab:frontier-scb}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{\begin{tabular}{@{}llrrrrl@{}}
\toprule
Model & Ext. & SC (\%) & $n_\text{err}$ & BR & $\Delta$ (pp) \\
\midrule
Mistral-7B & $^a$ & 55.88 & 90 & 4.97 & 0.00 \\
LLaMA-8B & $^a$ & \textrm{---} & \textrm{---} & 2.88 & \textrm{---} \\
Qwen-14B & $^a$ & 75.49 & 50 & 3.93 & $-$0.98 \\
\addlinespace
R1-Distill-8B & $^b$ & 74.51 & 52 & 2.92 & $+$2.45 \\
gpt-4o & $^a$ & 73.91 & 53 & 2.94 & $-$1.80 \\
gpt-4.1 & $^b$ & 82.84 & 35 & 2.70 & $-$1.47 \\
gpt-5.5 & $^b$ & 84.31 & 32 & 3.48 & $+$0.49 \\
o3 & $^b$ & 82.65 & 35 & 1.66 & 0.00 \\
Gemini-2.5-Pro$^*$ & $^b$ & 87.37 & 12 & 2.79 & $-$1.10 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\looseness=-1 Two controls rule out answer leakage. BR is uniform across early, mid, and late reasoning steps (Fisher $p = 0.71$); high-consensus errors amplify to $\text{BR} = 18.8\times$ versus $2.7\times$ for low-consensus ($p < 0.001$). Removing the answer sentence from traces before extraction yields no BR decrease (Wilcoxon $p \geq 0.12$) despite significant accuracy loss (McNemar $p < 0.013$). SCB reflects a distributional prior embedded throughout reasoning, not answer contamination.

\subsection{Process-Level Independence}
\label{sec:process-kappa}

\looseness=-1 We quantify reasoning-process overlap via ProcessSim, the mean pairwise cosine similarity of TF-IDF trace vectors (Eq.~\ref{eq:process-sim}, Appendix~\ref{app:process-gradient}). ProcessSim captures lexical overlap; deeper structure measures may reveal additional patterns, so we treat it as supporting rather than primary evidence. On Mistral-7B FOLIO, mean ProcessSim $= 0.69$, far exceeding answer-level $\kappa = 0.46$: traces share reasoning processes even when answers diverge. The gap generalizes across four families (Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-process}), from $0.31$ (LLaMA) to $0.02$ (Qwen-14B). Under cross-model extraction, SICA gain decreases monotonically with ProcessSim ($\rho = -1.0$, $p = 0.008$; Table~\ref{tab:process-gradient}), modulated by self-bias severity.


CCR analysis confirms the same pattern: cross-architecture NLI yields $\text{CCR} > 0$ while same-model SICA yields $\text{CCR} < 0$, with CCR correlating with combo gain ($\rho = 0.84$, $p = 0.014$; Appendix~\ref{app:cil}).

\subsection{Remediation Analysis}
\label{sec:diagnostics}
\label{sec:remediation}
\label{sec:interventions}

\looseness=-1 All 27 training-free remediation strategies spanning four categories produce neutral-to-negative results (Figure~\ref{fig:interventions-summary}; Appendices~\ref{app:extraction}--\ref{app:entropy}). Four representative failure modes illuminate the independence-accuracy trade-off. \emph{Debate} ($\kappa = 0.15$, $\Delta = -18.6$\,pp) successfully breaks trace correlation but collapses accuracy---same-model diversity and quality are at odds. \emph{Cross-model extraction} (using a different LLM as extractor) decreases $\kappa$ slightly but $\Delta \approx 0$, because same-family models share systematic biases (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:kappa_generalization}). \emph{Contrastive prompting} produces imbalanced output (94 pro vs.\ 65 con constraints), indicating confirmation bias transfers to constraint generation. The \emph{oracle ceiling} ($+29.90$\,pp with gold constraints) proves the mechanism is sound; self-extraction captures only ${\sim}$12\%, localizing the bottleneck to extraction quality. All non-debate strategies cluster at $\kappa \approx 0.40$--$0.47$, a same-model ceiling across three families. No individual strategy achieves significance after correction (Appendix~\ref{app:intervention-individual}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_interventions_compact.pdf}
\caption{Twenty-three of 27 training-free strategies across four categories (four additional strategies tested but omitted for clarity). All produce neutral-to-negative $\Delta$ vs.\ SC. Grey band: $\pm 1$\,pp noise zone.}
\label{fig:interventions-summary}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Symbolic Mirage Hypothesis: verification.}
\looseness=-1 The observations and prediction of \S\ref{sec:mirage} are confirmed. Observations O1--O2, initially discovered on Mistral-7B, generalize: BR $> 1$ across three self-extraction models, six cross-model pairs, and all eight cells of a $2{\times}2$ generator${\times}$extractor matrix (\S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias}); 27 training-free strategies fail to reduce BR below 1.0 (\S\ref{sec:remediation}). Prediction P1 is validated: cross-architecture NLI escapes the mirage for generators up to SC $\approx 70\%$ (\S\ref{sec:cross-arch}).

\vspace{-2mm}
\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}

\looseness=-1 We identify symbolic confirmation bias: self-extracted constraints inherit the generating model's biases ($\text{BR} = 4.97\times$, 83.3\%), explaining why twenty-seven remediation strategies fail. Oracle controls ($+29.90$/$+14.22$\,pp) confirm the mechanism is sound; the bottleneck is extraction quality, not the formal channel. Three diagnostics predict failure ($C_0 < 5\%$, SC $\leq 40\%$, process similarity). Cross-architecture NLI escapes the bias for generators up to SC $\approx 70\%$ ($p \leq 0.004$); DRC recovers 83--90\% of this gain at 35--39\% cost.

